      "completion_tokens": 1125,
      "output": "**Water** is a transparent, tasteless, odorless, and nearly colorless chemical substance that is the main constituent of Earth's hydrosphere and the fluids of all known living organisms. It is essential for all known forms of life.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is from chemical, physical, biological, and planetary perspectives:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Chemical Identity\n*   **Molecular Formula:** **H₂O** (two hydrogen atoms covalently bonded to one oxygen atom).\n*   **Molecular Structure:** The molecule is **bent (V-shaped)** at an angle of ~104.5°. Because oxygen is more electronegative than hydrogen, the molecule is **polar**—it has a partial negative charge near the oxygen and partial positive charges near the hydrogens.\n*   **Hydrogen Bonding:** This polarity allows water molecules to stick to one another via **hydrogen bonds**. This relatively weak but abundant intermolecular force is responsible for almost all of water’s unique properties.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Unique Physical Properties (Caused by Hydrogen Bonding)\nWater behaves differently than almost any other substance on Earth:\n\n| Property | Description | Why It Matters |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Universal Solvent** | Dissolves more substances than any other liquid. | Transports nutrients in biology; shapes geology (caves, canyons). |\n| **Density Anomaly** | **Ice floats.** Water is densest at 4°C (39°F); it expands when freezing. | Insulates lakes/oceans in winter, allowing life to survive underneath. |\n| **High Specific Heat** | Absorbs a lot of heat before temperature rises. | Stabilizes Earth’s climate; regulates body temperature in organisms. |\n| **High Heat of Vaporization** | Takes massive energy to turn liquid to gas. | Sweating/evaporation cools organisms efficiently. |\n| **Cohesion & Adhesion** | Sticks to itself (cohesion) and other surfaces (adhesion). | **Capillary action** moves water up tree trunks (xylem) against gravity. |\n| **Surface Tension** | High surface tension creates a \"skin\" on the surface. | Allows insects (water striders) to walk on water. |\n\n---\n\n### 3. States of Matter (Phases)\nWater is the only common substance to exist naturally in all three states within Earth's normal temperature range:\n1.  **Solid (Ice):** Crystalline structure (hexagonal), less dense than liquid.\n2.  **Liquid (Water):** The standard state at room temperature; dynamic hydrogen bond network.\n3.  **Gas (Water Vapor/Steam):** Invisible gas; a potent greenhouse gas.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Biological Role: The \"Matrix of Life\"\n*   **Solvent for Metabolism:** Dissolves ions, proteins, DNA, and sugars, enabling chemical reactions (metabolism) to occur in solution.\n*   **Transport Medium:** Blood (mostly water) transports oxygen, nutrients, hormones, and waste.\n*   **Structural Support:** **Turgor pressure** (water pressure inside cells) keeps plants upright and gives cells shape.\n*   **Reactant:** Essential for **hydrolysis** (breaking bonds using water) and **photosynthesis** ($6CO_2 + 6H_2O \\rightarrow C_6H_{12}O_6 + 6O_2$).\n*   **Temperature Buffer:** High heat capacity prevents rapid temperature spikes in cells.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Planetary Context (The Hydrosphere)\n*   **Coverage:** ~71% of Earth's surface.\n*   **Distribution:**\n    *   **97.5% Saline** (Oceans).\n    *   **2.5% Freshwater** (mostly locked in ice caps/glaciers/groundwater; <0.3% easily accessible in lakes/rivers).\n*   **The Water Cycle (Hydrologic Cycle):** Continuous movement via evaporation, transpiration, condensation, precipitation, and runoff. This cycle distributes fresh water globally and drives weather systems.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Human Use & Significance\n*   **Agriculture:** ~70% of global freshwater withdrawals (irrigation).\n*   **Industry:** Cooling, processing, cleaning, hydroelectric power (~19%).\n*   **Domestic:** Drinking, sanitation, hygiene (~10%).\n*   **Geopolitics:** \"Water scarcity\" and transboundary river disputes are major 21st-century security concerns.\n\n---\n\n### Summary Definition\n> **Water is a small, polar molecule (H₂O) whose extensive hydrogen bonding creates anomalous physical properties—most notably a solid phase less dense than its liquid phase and a high capacity to store heat and dissolve matter. These properties make it the indispensable solvent, transport medium, thermal buffer, and structural agent for all known biology, while simultaneously driving Earth's climate and geology.**"
